WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.571
SQL injection remains a common example because the attack changes the meaning of a database query.

2
00:00:06.571 --> 00:00:13.477
The same pattern can appear anywhere user input is mixed into an interpreter without strong boundaries.

3
00:00:13.477 --> 00:00:27.892
Command injection is especially dangerous because it can let an attacker execute operating system commands through application features such as file conversion, diagnostics, import jobs, or administrative utilities.

4
00:00:27.892 --> 00:00:39.492
LDAP, NoSQL, template, expression language, and other injection types follow the same core failure: data crosses a trust boundary and becomes executable or structural input.

5
00:00:39.492 --> 00:00:43.783
Output encoding and input validation are complementary controls.

6
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:52.835
Validation rejects input that does not belong, while encoding and parameterization preserve the boundary between data and instructions.

7
00:00:52.835 --> 00:00:57.729
Use parameterized queries and safe APIs instead of string-built commands.

8
00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:01.149
Validate input against expected formats and ranges.

9
00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:06.647
Encode output for the correct context and handle errors without leaking internals.

10
00:01:06.647 --> 00:01:17.442
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SI-10 - Information Input Validation; SI-11 - Error Handling; SI-7 - Software, Firmware, and Information Integrity.

